<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-browser</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         WITHOUT THIS THE PAGE LOADS AND THE GAME NEVER APPEARS.

         Avalonia draws through Skia, and in WebAssembly Skia is not a library that can be
         loaded at run time - there is no dynamic linker to load it with. libSkiaSharp.a
         has to be RELINKED INTO THE WASM BINARY at build time, and that only happens when
         this is true.

         With it false the build SUCCEEDS, emits a warning nobody reads -

             @(NativeFileReference) is not empty, but the native references won't be
             linked in, because neither $(WasmBuildNative), nor $(RunAOTCompilation)
             are 'true'

         - and produces a page that shows the Avalonia splash, then dies in the browser
         console with `System.DllNotFoundException: libSkiaSharp` at SKImageInfo's static
         constructor. Nothing on the page says so. That is how this head sat "built" and
         unrunnable: the only signal was a warning in a build that reported 0 errors.

         THE COST IS A WORKLOAD. Relinking needs Emscripten, which arrives with
         `dotnet workload install wasm-tools`; without it the build falls back to the
         warning above rather than failing. See BROWSER.md.
         ================================================================== -->
    <WasmBuildNative>true</WasmBuildNative>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Jones.App\Jones.App.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       THE ART, PUBLISHED BESIDE THE PAGE.

       Without it this head draws the 838 cels of Sierra's 1x art compiled into
       Jones.App - which is what it did for its whole existence, because a head that
       finds no art falls back silently and falling back looks exactly like working.

       NOT EMBEDDED, NOT BUNDLED, JUST FILES. The 4x set is 57MB across 1203 cels and a
       player opens a handful of screens, so HttpArtStore fetches each one the first time
       it is drawn. Embedding them in the assembly would put all 57MB in the initial
       download; bundling them in a zip would mean fetching and holding all of it to read
       any of it.

       BOTH DIRECTORIES OR NEITHER. `png4x-cast0` holds the cels we have rebuilt and
       `png4x` holds all 1071; a set is silent about what it does not replace and the
       resolver falls through to the ported copy, so shipping the set alone would draw
       132 cels at 4x beside 939 at 1x.

       THE MANIFEST IS NOT A CONVENIENCE. HTTP has no directory listing, and a set is
       silent about nine cels in ten, so without it every miss would cost a round trip to
       learn that the art set does not cover that cel. One text file, written here,
       read once at startup.
       ================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <JonesAssetRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\..\assets\</JonesAssetRoot>
    <JonesArtOut>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)wwwroot\art\</JonesArtOut>
    <JonesArtManifest>$(JonesArtOut)art-manifest.txt</JonesArtManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <JonesArtFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)png4x\*.png"       ArtDir="png4x" />
    <JonesArtFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)png4x-cast0\*.png" ArtDir="png4x-cast0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THIS PROJECT FILE IS AN INPUT, and leaving it out is a real bug that has already
    shipped once in this repo: with only the files as inputs, REMOVING a line leaves every
    remaining input older than the output, so MSBuild judges the target up to date and
    publishes the previous set. See the Android csproj for the incident.
  -->
  <Target Name="PublishJonesArt" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild"
          Inputs="@(JonesArtFile);$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)" Outputs="$(JonesArtManifest)">
    <RemoveDir Directories="$(JonesArtOut)" />
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(JonesArtFile)"
          DestinationFiles="@(JonesArtFile->'$(JonesArtOut)%(ArtDir)\%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(JonesArtManifest)"
                      Lines="@(JonesArtFile->'%(ArtDir)/%(Filename)%(Extension)')"
                      Overwrite="true" WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />
  </Target>

  <!-- NO <Content Include="wwwroot\art\**"> HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.

       The WebAssembly SDK already globs wwwroot into Content, so declaring it again is a
       duplicate - NETSDK1022, which the Debug BUILD tolerates and `publish` refuses. The
       art lands under wwwroot above and is carried to the publish output by that default
       glob; the only thing an explicit item adds is a build that works until you try to
       deploy it. -->
</Project>
